Lifecycle-first handoff reproduction
===================================

This branch is test-only integration, not the #23--#25 submission. It contains
the separate #26 loader and explicitly temporary modern #27/#28 adapters.
Do not merge it as production support for those issues.

The writer-stress recipe runs a real two-hart child, then 64 barrier-paired
active-unload attempts and 64 MKTTY open/tagged-ping/read/close operations.
Each unload must return EBUSY. Each console must receive its unique tagged
response before release; a second ping is sent immediately before close to
exercise receive/release timing. Both workers must finish, ownership must
remain unchanged, and ordinary halt must confirm all assigned harts STOPPED
before unloading and returning their resources. Unsupported RISC-V force-halt
must return EOPNOTSUPP while preserving ACTIVE state and ownership. This is
refusal qualification, not supported remote force-stop behavior. The first connection is
closed before stress so it cannot mask receive on the temporary connections.

Run Python fixture checks from the repository root:

  python3 -B -m unittest discover -s tools/testing/selftests/multikernel -p 'test_*.py'

After building this exact clean revision (RV64 MULTIKERNEL/MKTTY/KPROBES), use:

  bash tools/testing/selftests/multikernel/qemu_lifecycle_test.sh \
    KERNEL_BUILD HARNESS_CHECKOUT KERF_CHECKOUT RISCV_RUNTIME_ROOT \
    HOST_BOUND_FW_DYNAMIC_BIN OUTPUT_PARENT/build writer-stress 2 1

Dependencies: #35 harness48507f176520b11a655344510b1b865b037e527f,
Kerf38daa5687d6748846ffd003599a3bfba29785cd6. The harness and Kerf checkouts
must be clean. Supply the RISC-V runtime root prepared using the pinned #35
packing flow. Firmware must be rebuilt/bound to this exact host ELF/Image;
the integration priming address is derived from the ELF, not copied from an
older evidence bundle. See opensbi_fault_build.sh for host-bound firmware.

The recipe preserves the exact command, serial log, source revision, config,
Images/ELFs, verifier, dependency pins and SHA256SUMS in a fresh bundle.
Verifier failure or nonzero exit is never a pass.

This is bounded native stress, not forced kernel interleaving, callback
unregistration/RCU grace-period qualification, or incoming CPU-worker stress.
It does not establish AIA/device filtering, real domain denial, DMA quiescence,
mixed-version ACK compatibility, or a physical stale-I-cache hazard result.
